 "title": "Sync API vs queue",
 "sources": [
  "Amazon SQS Developer Guide, 'What is Amazon SQS' (official, fetched 2026-11-17): SQS offers a secure, durable, and available hosted queue that lets you integrate and decouple distributed software systems and components. https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html",
  "Amazon SQS Developer Guide, 'Amazon SQS standard queues' (official, fetched 2026-11-17): standard queues ensure at-least-once delivery, more than one copy of a message might be delivered, messages may occasionally arrive out of order; they make a best-effort attempt to keep the order messages were sent; suitable as long as your application can handle messages that arrive more than once or out of order. https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/standard-queues.html",
  "Illustration, not a quoted case: the sign-up email example and its 30 seconds are made-up example numbers, labelled on screen."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "A user clicks sign up, and your server sends the welcome email before answering, so they stare at a spinner.",
   "caps": "*SIGN UP | SERVER SENDS *EMAIL FIRST | USER *WAITS"
  },
  {
   "text": "A message queue fixes that: the server drops a message in the queue and answers right away.",
   "caps": "A *QUEUE | DROP *A MESSAGE | ANSWER *INSTANTLY"
  },
  {
   "text": "A separate worker picks the message up and sends the email in the background.",
   "caps": "A *WORKER | PICKS IT UP | SENDS IN THE *BACKGROUND"
  },
  {
   "text": "That decouples the two sides. Amazon describes SQS as a hosted queue that decouples distributed systems.",
   "caps": "*DECOUPLED | A HOSTED *QUEUE | SQS"
  },
  {
   "text": "The user gets an instant response, and the slow work happens whenever the worker gets to it.",
   "caps": "*INSTANT RESPONSE | SLOW WORK *LATER"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: standard SQS queues deliver at least once, so a message can arrive twice, or out of order.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | AT LEAST *ONCE | CAN ARRIVE *TWICE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So your worker has to handle duplicates, for example by checking whether it already sent that email.",
   "caps": "HANDLE *DUPLICATES | CHECK *ALREADY SENT"
  },
  {
   "text": "So queue the slow, non-urgent work, and keep anything the user needs to see right now synchronous.",
   "caps": "QUEUE THE *SLOW WORK | KEEP *URGENT SYNC"
  }
 ],
